0:00:19.175,0:00:22.295
It is March 3rd, 1997.

0:00:22.375,0:00:24.375
I'm speaking with Jonas Stemmer.

0:00:24.375,0:00:26.454
The interviewer is Peter Engelsman.

0:00:26.535,0:00:29.015
We are in Scheveningen, the Netherlands.

0:00:29.015,0:00:30.960
The language is Dutch.

0:00:31.359,0:00:32.960
Should we have the letter?

0:00:34.159,0:00:35.679
Can you say who you are?

0:00:37.200,0:00:39.520
My name is Jonas Stemmer.

0:00:40.159,0:00:45.119
I was born on November 7th, 1917 in Amsterdam.

0:00:46.445,0:00:47.725
Where in Amsterdam?

0:00:48.125,0:00:49.884
Hartenstraat 19.

0:00:51.085,0:00:53.405
What kind of neighborhood was that back then?

0:00:56.445,0:01:08.629
Well, Hartenstraat is a side street of the Herengracht. That's not far from the palace on the Dam.

0:01:12.869,0:01:17.915
My father had... we had a building there, a residence.

0:01:17.995,0:01:25.834
Downstairs we had a shop with watches and gold and silver, I think.

0:01:41.257,0:01:53.614
In 1919, end of 1919 or 1920, we went back to Germany, where my parents had lived since 1901.

0:01:53.614,0:01:54.960
Where did you go?

0:01:54.974,0:01:57.055
To Ludwigshafen am Rhein.

0:01:57.215,0:01:59.135
And you were 2 or 3 years old then?

0:01:59.135,0:02:01.614
I was 3 years old then.

0:02:02.500,0:02:04.500
How big was your family?

0:02:04.500,0:02:10.819
We were three brothers and one sister.

0:02:13.073,0:02:18.188
My eldest brother was born in 1901

0:02:18.376,0:02:36.409
My second brother, Mordechai, was born in 1906, my sister Rosa was born in 1910. Both of them were born in Ludwigshafen, and I in 1917.

0:02:36.409,0:02:38.650
I was the youngest.

0:02:40.889,0:02:43.210
Can you remember going there?

0:02:44.625,0:02:45.185
To Germany?

0:02:46.224,0:02:50.465
Later, maybe when I was 5 or 6 years old, I can remember something.

0:02:55.004,0:03:03.483
My brother got married in Nuremberg.

0:03:03.483,0:03:07.000
I remember once, we were there at a celebration.

0:03:07.800,0:03:19.720
I think it was the Brit Milah of their first son. We came back through Mannheim.

0:03:20.000,0:03:27.480
That was the town opposite Ludwigshafen. 

0:03:27.637,0:03:36.640
And we had to walk from Mannheim to Ludwigshafen because the French occupation troops 

0:03:36.640,0:03:40.210
were going from the right bank to the left bank of the Rhine.

0:03:40.210,0:03:46.185
Along the way, as my parents always told, I got lost. They lost me.

0:03:46.824,0:03:52.905
In the evening, someone brought me home. I was 6 years old then.

0:03:53.224,0:03:58.815
My oldest nephew is 6 years younger than me.

0:03:59.340,0:04:02.540
What kind of place was Ludwigshafen back then?

0:04:03.340,0:04:03.980
What do you say?

0:04:03.980,0:04:04.699
What kind of place

0:04:04.699,0:04:05.819
was Ludwigshafen?

0:04:05.819,0:04:09.980
Ludwigshafen was a large and very well-known IG Farben industrial area.

0:04:11.500,0:04:12.860
A major industrial town.

0:04:13.634,0:04:18.579
About 120,000 people lived there then in Ludwigshafen.

0:04:20.435,0:04:22.435
Why did your father go back to Germany?

0:04:23.714,0:04:28.834
Because we had lived there, he had business there and thought, well, I can start again.

0:04:29.129,0:04:31.370
But was it more difficult in the Netherlands?

0:04:31.370,0:04:34.970
It seems so, it seems so. I don't know.

0:04:34.970,0:04:37.850
Why they actually went back, I don't know.

0:04:37.850,0:04:47.745
They went back and it became difficult anyway, because my father became a Polish citizen.

0:04:48.305,0:04:49.025
Polish?

0:04:49.025,0:05:01.650
He became Polish because he was born, I told you, he was born in Oświęcim. That's what later became the terrible place, Auschwitz.

0:05:06.770,0:05:16.764
And then it became difficult for him to travel, because we also had a business in Worms, that's also a well-known place.

0:05:19.725,0:05:28.100
And then he still didn't want to, because there was a strike. The Germans who were on strike didn't want to go by train. They stopped the trains running.

0:05:28.419,0:05:35.620
The French occupation forces took care of it, so my father could possibly travel.

0:05:35.620,0:05:37.379
But he didn't do that, of course.

0:05:37.620,0:05:41.144
It became very difficult, apparently.

0:05:41.944,0:05:43.625
We did business.

0:05:46.264,0:05:59.399
There was already a lot of anti-Semitism there at the time. It was actually the Pfalz in Bavaria. There was already quite a bit of anti-Semitism in those places everywhere.

0:05:59.399,0:06:03.160
Your father was still in watches and clocks?

0:06:03.160,0:06:03.800
Yes.

0:06:05.160,0:06:05.660
Everything.

0:06:06.305,0:06:13.985
Everything, we had silver and gold and they even did business with IG Farben indeed.

0:06:15.344,0:06:23.530
Waste products, those we had. My father even had an oven where he could melt gold in.

0:06:28.760,0:06:29.613
Where did you go to school?

0:06:29.801,0:06:31.689
In Ludwigshafen, at the Rhein School.

0:06:32.824,0:06:44.211
Yes, that's actually where I was. In 19... end of 1929, that was the crisis time in Germany.

0:06:45.699,0:06:53.620
So my eldest brother, he married in Nuremberg, and my second brother, he married in Berlin.

0:06:55.779,0:07:06.925
And then we went to Berlin, at the end of 1929, beginning of 1930.

0:07:09.084,0:07:11.259
You mean you moved then?

0:07:11.259,0:07:13.259
From Ludwigshafen to Berlin.

0:07:13.259,0:07:15.100
Why was that?

0:07:15.899,0:07:23.985
Probably due to economic difficulties. That there was no bread anymore. It was difficult.

0:07:24.785,0:07:31.985
Did you feel as a child, if you can know that, German or Polish or Dutch?

0:07:33.745,0:07:43.500
I went to school, I can remember that. We lived in Ludwigshafen on Maxstraße 7.

0:07:44.939,0:07:53.285
I can remember, how old was I then when we went to Berlin? So from 1929, then I was 12 years old.

0:07:53.285,0:07:53.785
Then

0:07:57.444,0:08:06.564
Then they were already marching, the Nazis, the SA, I don't know what it was, with swastika flags through our street.

0:08:07.850,0:08:12.730
Something that always stays before my eyes.

0:08:13.129,0:08:15.370
How was it at school? Did you have Jewish friends?

0:08:18.250,0:08:27.928
At school, I don't know. We had a Jewish school. We attended Jewish school, from the Jewish community. 

0:08:27.928,0:08:37.840
That was lessons on Wednesday, I'm not even sure. Then we got Jewish lessons.

0:08:37.840,0:08:45.350
But we also had... I also went to, that was the Eastern Jewish community, everything was small, they had what you call a cheder.

0:08:46.294,0:08:48.294
And we learned there.

0:08:49.574,0:08:54.695
Our parents wanted us to know something about Judaism.

0:08:54.855,0:08:56.214
Were they very religious at home?

0:08:56.214,0:08:57.894
We were very orthodox at that time.

0:09:00.214,0:09:21.804
My brothers... I can tell you that in the meantime, from 1916 until we went back, my second brother, Marcus, or Mordechai, was at the seminary in Amsterdam.

0:09:22.125,0:09:30.684
My older brother was called Salomon and he helped in the business.

0:09:33.132,0:09:41.213
He traveled around the countryside and bought those... those caps.

0:09:45.002,0:09:49.758
From Zeeland.

0:09:49.774,0:09:50.575
In the Netherlands.

0:09:51.054,0:09:51.855
In Zeeland.

0:09:52.095,0:09:52.654
Zeeland.

0:09:53.695,0:09:57.294
Yes. That iron, that was gold.

0:09:57.375,0:09:58.495
Gold or silver.

0:09:58.995,0:10:00.208
It was indeed gold and silver.

0:10:00.860,0:10:04.251
They call them ear irons. Ear irons yes.

0:10:09.755,0:10:11.755
That was.

0:10:12.693,0:10:16.491
Yes, that's where he learned his lesson, my father, my father that is.

0:10:16.491,0:10:19.904
We actually didn't live in Amsterdam in the Jewish quarter.

0:10:20.225,0:10:34.850
It was a bit. My father went to shul. Abel Herzberg wrote a letter about it, letters to my grandson.

0:10:35.169,0:10:40.690
And he describes that shul in the Nieuwe Kerkstraat. That was the so-called Russian Shul.

0:10:42.304,0:10:48.384
My father prayed there every Shabbat there.

0:10:49.985,0:10:53.664
We had many, many acquaintances and friends in Amsterdam.

0:10:57.779,0:10:59.059
How was Berlin?

0:11:00.340,0:11:00.980
Berlin,

0:11:00.980,0:11:03.860
we had a very difficult time there.

0:11:03.860,0:11:06.580
We also lived there with a small shop.

0:11:09.345,0:11:18.784
On Gipsstrasse, also in the center of the city, near the Hackescher Markt.

0:11:21.710,0:11:23.710
I've never been back there.

0:11:26.350,0:11:40.995
A small business, but my mother, even in Ludwigshafen, my mother went into the countryside, also in Ludwigshafen.

0:11:44.595,0:11:55.610
In the small villages there, she collected watches and alarm clocks.

0:11:56.970,0:12:01.705
My father repaired them at home. What was the reason?

0:12:01.705,0:12:05.225
The reason was that he was afraid. He was afraid to go into the countryside.

0:12:05.225,0:12:09.601
It was a difficult time.

0:12:09.788,0:12:11.545
But why was he afraid?

0:12:11.545,0:12:14.183
He had a beard. My father.

0:12:17.919,0:12:20.559
You'd hear all kinds of things.

0:12:20.960,0:12:24.960
That was in the 1920s.

0:12:27.365,0:12:32.245
When you went from the Netherlands to Germany, did you have to learn German?

0:12:33.284,0:12:35.044
I was still a child.

0:12:36.164,0:12:38.725
I had Dutch, but I don't remember anymore.

0:12:39.365,0:12:40.324
I learned it at the time.

0:12:40.324,0:12:45.430
We arrived, and then Hitler came.

0:12:46.389,0:12:51.750
January 30th, 1933, Hitler came.

0:12:54.070,0:12:55.207
You were still in Berlin then.

0:12:56.284,0:13:04.560
The reason we came to Holland was because of my brother, Marcus who lived in Berlin. 

0:13:12.712,0:13:25.414
In April, or was it March, maybe, 1933, he was beaten up beaten into a pulp in the courthouse in Berlin.

0:13:25.414,0:13:28.375
Berlin Mitte, they called it, the center.

0:13:29.894,0:13:40.149
He came to his in-laws more dead than alive. He couldn't go home.

0:13:40.149,0:13:42.710
And he was lying on a couch.

0:13:42.710,0:13:43.910
How did that happen?

0:13:44.309,0:13:45.509
How did that happen?

0:13:45.670,0:13:49.269
He had to be there. He had to be there for something.

0:13:50.804,0:13:53.524
And the SA came in there.

0:13:57.125,0:14:00.804
I have a photo. There's also a photo of my brother.

0:14:04.084,0:14:11.400
And he couldn't walk or stand. 

0:14:12.600,0:14:19.400
He didn't want to tell my parents what had happened. And he said, 'I'm going to my brother.' 

0:14:19.400,0:14:27.200
In the meantime, my brother from Nuremberg had already left in 1932

0:14:27.600,0:14:35.760
He said, I'm going, I'm going to Holland. The Hague. To live

0:14:38.760,0:14:42.124
I'm going to Holland to my brother.

0:14:46.120,0:14:46.920
Good.

0:14:48.318,0:14:59.551
Only when he was already in Holland... It was still easier to travel by train in 1933.

0:15:00.639,0:15:04.320
Only then did we learn what had happened to him.

0:15:07.120,0:15:19.785
Then the SA came to our house. His wife and child... We only have one child. He's a professor in Jerusalem.

0:15:20.904,0:15:26.345
And they asked, 'Where is Marcus Stemmer?'

0:15:26.870,0:15:29.669
'I don't know. I don't know where he is.'

0:15:29.669,0:15:33.110
Well, that happened two, three times. They kept coming back.

0:15:33.110,0:15:34.389
They had to have him.

0:15:35.990,0:15:39.429
So for us, we couldn't stay anymore.

0:15:40.565,0:15:47.445
So we did the same thing. We also took the train to the Netherlands.

0:15:48.325,0:15:53.360
The train ticket was for Berlin to Antwerp.

0:15:54.879,0:16:07.475
At the border at Oldenzaal, our brother who already lived here came to meet us.

0:16:08.195,0:16:14.595
And he said, 'You get off here,' and we took another train to The Hague.

0:16:14.835,0:16:16.514
Then we came to The Hague.

0:16:16.514,0:16:17.955
What could you take with you?

0:16:18.115,0:16:18.615
Nothing.

0:16:21.250,0:16:23.889
Maybe a suitcase or something?

0:16:24.290,0:16:25.810
No, probably.

0:16:26.050,0:16:35.115
Just my father, my mother, and me. The three of us.

0:16:35.995,0:16:38.634
My sister was already married by then.

0:16:40.475,0:16:55.680
She also lived in Berlin. She was married with Warhaftig. And they came to the Netherlands a few months later as well.

0:16:56.160,0:17:00.639
The house you lived in, did it just stay there?

0:17:00.894,0:17:02.014
Yes, that's how it was.

0:17:02.014,0:17:07.775
We lived on the second floor on that street.

0:17:07.775,0:17:09.855
And here, we got a new house.

0:17:09.934,0:17:11.694
But furniture and those kinds of things?

0:17:12.734,0:17:13.694
Just left behind.

0:17:13.694,0:17:14.194
Just left behind.

0:17:18.539,0:17:26.859
I do know that at first, the first address we had here, where we got a home.

0:17:27.420,0:17:29.660
Housing was very cheap here at the time.

0:17:30.394,0:17:43.355
On Schelderstraat. Later we lived on Radiolaan. Then everything was gone.

0:17:44.220,0:17:46.859
So you were taken in by your brother?

0:17:46.859,0:17:48.539
We were taken in by my brother.

0:17:48.539,0:17:50.139
And he took you to Amsterdam?

0:17:50.380,0:17:52.779
No, he also lived here in The Hague.

0:17:53.500,0:17:55.019
Yes, you came to The Hague?

0:17:55.019,0:17:56.299
Yes, came to The Hague.

0:17:56.299,0:18:03.424
My brother came to his brother-in-law who had a clothing business, women's clothing.

0:18:06.945,0:18:09.904
I worked there for a while.

0:18:10.705,0:18:15.839
First I tried something else because I already wanted to go to Palestine then.

0:18:17.039,0:18:25.200
That didn't work. It was very difficult because you couldn't get a certificate, and then you had to go to hachshara.

0:18:25.440,0:18:27.680
It was all very complicated.

0:18:30.174,0:18:36.095
In the meantime, my mother had died young, 54 years old.

0:18:37.455,0:18:46.309
I was still working with my brother, in the business.

0:18:46.309,0:18:47.829
How did that work?

0:18:47.829,0:18:54.869
I mean, you come from Berlin, you come to The Hague, you get a house, there's nothing in it, you have nothing.

0:18:54.869,0:18:57.190
How does

0:18:57.190,0:18:58.470
that work? You have to sleep?

0:18:59.750,0:19:08.515
It was fine. Well, that was fine, you could get that. Buy a few beds, a couple tables and chairs. How much do you need?

0:19:08.595,0:19:09.875
No, you don't need much.

0:19:09.875,0:19:17.429
I don't know if they had anything sent from Berlin. I can hardly imagine it.

0:19:18.309,0:19:20.549
I can't remember. I don't know anymore.

0:19:20.710,0:19:21.210
But

0:19:22.069,0:19:24.230
you could get work pretty easily, at least?

0:19:24.230,0:19:26.150
Well, not easily at all to get work.

0:19:26.150,0:19:26.950
I worked with my brother, yes.

0:19:26.950,0:19:27.910
With my brother, yes.

0:19:31.975,0:19:34.600
I did anything and everything, helping with machines.

0:19:35.600,0:19:46.600
Textiles, clothing. I believe mainly women's clothing. 

0:19:46.600,0:19:56.200
And at the end of '37 I got engaged, beginning of '38 maybe, end of '37.

0:20:00.200,0:20:10.769
But I didn't have, the salary you got was so minimal. Then you can't start a family.

0:20:10.769,0:20:13.169
So I was still waiting.

0:20:13.809,0:20:15.329
Where did you meet your wife?

0:20:16.609,0:20:20.049
Here in Scheveningen. They had always lived in Scheveningen.

0:20:21.025,0:20:23.345
And they were members of the Jewish community and you saw them around?

0:20:23.345,0:20:24.144
Yes.

0:20:24.384,0:20:29.825
The Jewish community, at my parents-in-law was a bakery and a shop, also on Gentsestraat.

0:20:29.825,0:20:32.224
The bakery was on IJmuiderstraat.

0:20:34.460,0:20:36.619
And that's how I met my wife.

0:20:40.220,0:20:53.634
Meanwhile it was all a tense time. You know what all happened: '37, '38 then with the Anschluss, and so on and so on.

0:20:57.550,0:20:58.050
Kristalnacht

0:21:14.865,0:21:21.024
When it was almost '39, it became very difficult.

0:21:22.144,0:21:26.544
The threat of war was very great. At least, that's what I saw.

0:21:28.329,0:21:31.289
My parents-in-law, for example, didn't see that at all.

0:21:31.289,0:21:32.809
Not your father either?

0:21:32.809,0:21:34.649
My father did.

0:21:36.169,0:21:46.200
I forgot to tell that. What we experienced in Berlin and those things, until we fled.

0:21:46.200,0:21:59.200
On Shabbat we went from shul home, we walked through narrow alleys, with my brother-in-law and my father and me. I was still a little boy, I was 15 years old.

0:22:00.109,0:22:06.429
And then suddenly SA men came toward us.

0:22:07.630,0:22:09.149
My father had a beard.

0:22:13.951,0:22:17.233
It is a very narrow street, as wide as that room

0:22:21.200,0:22:26.640
He shouted at my father 'You Jew-swine, aren't you gone from here yet?!'

0:22:26.640,0:22:40.000
On a Sunday, I went to get something. Suddenly I saw on the street SA men who wore those white armbands with 'Hilfspolizei' written on them.

0:22:40.000,0:22:45.676
You know what those are? SA men, the brownshirts.

0:22:46.000,0:23:10.160
It was apparently a Jewish neighborhood, and they came from synagogue, or in any case, they encountered those people and beat them with rubber clubs, just like that on the street. Those are things that always stay with you.

0:23:11.215,0:23:33.819
Well, then in '33. My brother, yes, in '35 my second brother Marcus Mordecai went. He went with his wife and child, with his son and they got a certificate.

0:23:35.259,0:23:43.034
So it was also very interesting, because they got it. He had made a connection with someone from the English embassy.

0:23:43.515,0:23:50.154
That's how he got a certificate, because from the Jewish Agency it was very difficult to get the certificate.

0:23:53.210,0:23:56.970
He went to Palestine, lived there a whole time.

0:23:58.170,0:24:09.484
And in 1939, the war broke out. September, September 1st, 1939, right.

0:24:12.285,0:24:24.390
That brother-in-law of my brother who lived here, my oldest brother's brother-in-law, he already went - he was a Dutchman - he already went with his whole family to Chile.

0:24:25.509,0:24:30.444
Also my brother, he got his visa.

0:24:31.325,0:24:45.599
And he left December 4th, '39. December 4th, '39 to Chile by boat via Trieste, Italy.

0:24:51.960,0:24:52.460
Yes.

0:24:53.359,0:24:54.880
Yes, well how does it go with me?

0:24:57.305,0:25:14.890
Then it was, so they had a business and that was taken over by a firm in Rotterdam, Mr. Hartog, he took over that business and appointed me as manager in The Hague.

0:25:14.890,0:25:21.049
He had a business in Rotterdam. Still a very large business with or over 100 workers.

0:25:25.465,0:25:27.384
Here it was a smaller company.

0:25:27.384,0:25:30.105
Was that Mr. Hartog also in textiles?

0:25:30.105,0:25:34.744
Yes, yes, he had a large clothing factory in Rotterdam.

0:25:34.744,0:25:36.585
A well-known firm, Hartog.

0:25:38.799,0:25:41.519
A great man.

0:25:41.519,0:25:48.960
And I became manager and I got, a decent salary for then.

0:25:50.160,0:25:52.335
So I could marry.

0:25:54.494,0:26:07.190
And I stayed living in the building where that business was, because that was, it was actually property of my brother's brother-in-law.

0:26:08.470,0:26:10.390
And rent had to be paid there.

0:26:16.105,0:26:19.144
That was early 1940.

0:26:20.585,0:26:27.464
The chuppah, do you know what that is? I had the chuppah, December 31st, '39, the last day of the year.

0:26:28.950,0:26:30.549
The last day of the year.

0:26:34.630,0:26:38.950
Well, then I was the manager there in that building.

0:26:44.224,0:26:50.944
Early March '40.

0:26:52.384,0:26:55.184
Hartog said, 'Listen, this isn't going to work, you just have to move that whole business to Rotterdam.'

0:26:55.184,0:26:57.345
You have to move the whole business to Rotterdam.'

0:26:59.950,0:27:02.829
Well, that's not so easy for me.

0:27:07.230,0:27:12.075
And then I go to my parents-in-law, with my father, my sister, what should I do?

0:27:14.154,0:27:20.075
My parents-in-law said: try, maybe take over a part of that business.

0:27:20.555,0:27:42.804
And then, Mr. Hartog says to me: 'Jonas, you may have this and that of the machines, you may use all that. And then we'll see.

0:27:43.445,0:27:49.285
but I have to have this and this amount, there's no discussing that, you can pay it or you can't pay it.'

0:27:50.164,0:27:55.390
"I don't know. I'll consult with my parents-in-law, it wasn't a large amount."

0:27:56.350,0:28:01.309
I said, "Yes, I want to do it, but I need material to work with."

0:28:01.309,0:28:06.934
He said: 'Look, listen, you're first going to see if you can sell.

0:28:06.934,0:28:11.734
Just make a few models. If you can sell, then we'll look further.'

0:28:15.174,0:28:18.615
I got an order from Haas Fashion.

0:28:20.470,0:28:24.069
Have you ever heard of that? That it was the corner of the Spui. I believe 2 or 300 dresses.

0:28:25.269,0:28:43.924
He said, 'Look, the goods that are lying here, you can use those, but otherwise I'll send you goods from Rotterdam, and after three months you just pay me back.'

0:28:46.640,0:28:48.399
That Mr. Hartog could take over.

0:28:49.120,0:28:50.799
A large order from that Hague fashion house.

0:28:50.799,0:29:03.964
Yes, you can find it in the Chamber of Commerce. It's all written precisely that I took over a business from Mr. Hartog, and that was in April.

0:29:11.644,0:29:15.324
May 10th was a Friday morning.

0:29:18.479,0:29:25.199
We were in bed and it was a beautiful day.

0:29:26.479,0:29:29.519
And I'm walking and
I hear airplane engines.

0:29:31.155,0:29:35.315
I walk to the
window and I see an airplane.

0:29:37.715,0:29:41.875
I told my wife 'Do (Dora), it's war'.

0:29:45.810,0:29:48.289
She says, 'Jonas, you're crazy, go to bed.'

0:29:49.730,0:29:51.489
It was 4 o'clock in the morning.

0:29:54.209,0:30:12.239
Well, a few minutes later more came and I suddenly saw that cross - you know, the German one - on the airplane. Well, it's over. This is it. It's war.

0:30:13.359,0:30:19.200
And we had a distribution
radio then. Do you know what that is?

0:30:19.920,0:30:23.039
There was no one
then, because we had this.

0:30:23.905,0:30:27.584
This usually only came on at 7 o'clock.

0:30:29.185,0:30:33.025
I turned on the
radio and the radio worked.

0:30:33.665,0:30:44.780
And then you heard, 'Here is air service, here is air service'. They were announcing that enemy aircraft are coming.

0:30:46.140,0:30:50.299
In the area of The
Hague, parachutists have come down.

0:30:54.794,0:31:02.155
So into the street. I don't know
what else we did. No one came to the business.

0:31:02.155,0:31:07.594
We had that business. I
had about 6 or 7 girls working.

0:31:10.360,0:31:11.400
Were you scared?

0:31:12.360,0:31:13.560
Yes, I was scared.

0:31:14.439,0:31:19.879
Something else I'm forgetting.

0:31:20.599,0:31:26.474
Actually, I got civilly married on May 10th, 1939.

0:31:28.715,0:31:31.995
The reason was, I wanted to go to America.

0:31:33.594,0:31:38.009
And since I was born in
the Netherlands, it was the Dutch quota.

0:31:40.714,0:31:52.718
And my wife was born in the Sudetenland which belonged then... when she was born, I don't know if that belonged to Germany.

0:31:55.675,0:31:57.395
In any case, later it was Czechoslovakia.

0:32:00.428,0:32:04.995
So she couldn't get
in. The quota was closed.

0:32:06.590,0:32:15.150
But if we got married, then...
That's why we got married civilly on May 10th, 1939.

0:32:15.150,0:32:16.270
But that didn't work out?

0:32:16.750,0:32:17.950
It didn't work out, no.

0:32:19.514,0:32:20.875
We needed money.

0:32:23.354,0:32:35.270
Well, '40. We went to my parents to my parents-in-law we went, to Scheveningen.

0:32:37.030,0:32:42.870
We stayed there until May 14th.

0:32:46.775,0:33:03.189
We saw the pier we were standing there. Then you suddenly saw after 2 hours, between 2 and 3 o'clock, you already saw the smoke clouds from Rotterdam

0:33:03.189,0:33:05.270
Because Rotterdam was bombed.

0:33:05.350,0:33:08.870
Then Holland capitulated.

0:33:09.189,0:33:13.110
It was over at 4 o'clock,
but the bombardment was at 2 o'clock.

0:33:18.805,0:33:24.084
A few days later, we went back home.

0:33:24.324,0:33:26.324
We just went back to work.

0:33:26.404,0:33:27.524
The girls came.

0:33:28.470,0:33:34.629
I had a German girl - she was the cutter, a German girl.

0:33:41.990,0:33:50.514
We worked, and then
the Jewish decrees came.

0:33:50.514,0:33:55.235
No, it was 1942, 1941.

0:33:59.599,0:34:02.079
1940 was still fairly quiet.

0:34:02.800,0:34:06.959
In 1941, you had
to register your businesses.

0:34:09.625,0:34:22.985
You had to get your... the card what's it called the identity card.

0:34:27.389,0:34:41.185
The personal identification. First came the personal identification, I can remember. It was right near us. Next to where we lived there was a school, and there you could pick up the personal identification

0:34:43.904,0:34:51.745
I was sitting with a man who was
from the Netherlands, from the civil registry.

0:34:52.379,0:34:56.060
So everyone got that.
Everyone got a personal identification.

0:34:57.259,0:35:03.259
I was sitting with that man, and then with
the personal identification, you got a 'J' on it.

0:35:08.035,0:35:13.555
I was alone, so I only
had one for my wife and for me.

0:35:13.795,0:35:22.754
I said to that official, what I would never forget, I said... because he had all these stamps. There were stamps on it.

0:35:23.470,0:35:35.550
Yes, then that one still had to... it was already on the card, because they had to register, I think already in 1940. Jewish businesses had to be registered.

0:35:37.314,0:35:43.395
Then I said to that man, 'Listen, you don't need to put that on there, just leave it off.'

0:35:44.995,0:35:46.435
He looked at me as if I'm crazy.

0:35:49.529,0:35:51.690
Well, that was 1941.

0:35:51.930,0:36:03.529
During '41 it was. Then came the raids. In the winter, the February strike.

0:36:07.664,0:36:15.504
End of '41, several people fled from The Hague.

0:36:16.305,0:36:21.440
Several left alone without their families.

0:36:22.319,0:36:32.400
I had acquaintances, a friend from Amsterdam, Bernard de Leeuw, and he said, 'Boys, maybe we should leave too.'

0:36:35.024,0:36:36.144
I'm not leaving.

0:36:36.704,0:36:42.064
And then in 1941, my son was born on July 15th.

0:36:47.024,0:36:47.904
I'm not leaving.

0:36:51.839,0:37:11.875
Then came '42. It all went very quickly. The 24th - that was June 24th, I think, on a Friday.

0:37:17.479,0:37:20.120
I didn't get an administrator until then.

0:37:20.200,0:37:22.600
Most businesses already had an administrator.

0:37:23.080,0:37:24.279
I didn't have an administrator.

0:37:28.805,0:37:32.644
I got a phone call from the Wirtschaftsprüfstelle.

0:37:32.644,0:37:33.764
Do you know what that was?

0:37:33.845,0:37:38.884
It was an agency, a German agency, that was on the Korte Vijverberg here in The Hague.

0:37:39.685,0:37:51.889
And they sent a form every month during the course of '41, '42 to fill in whether we could do any work for the Wehrmacht.

0:37:52.450,0:37:56.529
And I kept writing back.

0:37:58.235,0:38:02.794
I have too little capacity, so I can't do that.

0:38:04.154,0:38:10.475
On that infamous Friday, that man calls. He says, 'Jonas Stemmer, ladies' clothing'.

0:38:12.519,0:38:16.759
I keep getting those forms back. Why is that?

0:38:18.679,0:38:22.759
He spoke in German, so I said, 'I don't understand German. What do you mean?'

0:38:25.674,0:38:27.674
That I always send it back.

0:38:27.674,0:38:32.714
Yes, I have a small business, so
the work I have is for those customers.

0:38:35.834,0:38:37.355
Then he said, 'Is it an Aryan business?'

0:38:39.100,0:38:40.059
Do you know what I mean?

0:38:42.523,0:38:46.539
No. That was Friday.

0:38:49.259,0:38:53.355
We made contact with various people because I wanted to leave.

0:38:54.394,0:38:58.394
Now is the time to try.

0:38:58.394,0:39:07.035
But yes, I had family here. My in-laws with 3 daughters, 2 grandchildren, twins of 3 years old.

0:39:07.839,0:39:13.679
I have my sister with 4
children, my father with my stepmother.

0:39:15.120,0:39:16.320
What should I do?

0:39:16.320,0:39:24.785
I want to leave because they called me
up for the work camps, so-called work camps.

0:39:28.225,0:39:38.559
I made contact with a woman in the city,
on Rembrandtstraat, who would bring us to Belgium.

0:39:40.319,0:39:55.054
And we left Wednesday morning, June 24th, 1942. It was a very warm day. A coat with some clothes and a bag.

0:39:55.694,0:39:57.214
A backpack. That was all.

0:39:58.574,0:40:03.134
We said we're going with
the star. We had to wear a star then.

0:40:04.014,0:40:05.054
It was all there then.

0:40:06.959,0:40:09.200
Only 2 months, but very short.

0:40:12.639,0:40:19.039
We didn't live far from the station, the
Holland Spoor station on the Nieuwe Schoolstraat.

0:40:21.435,0:40:32.955
And that woman had already bought the tickets, train tickets. And she took the child by the window because he was already 10 months old.

0:40:35.769,0:40:41.289
We walked behind each other, and on
the way to the station, we took off our coats.

0:40:41.769,0:40:45.529
Because you couldn't get
on the train with the star.

0:40:50.149,0:40:54.887
And then we arrived in Bergen op Zoom.

0:40:54.887,0:41:02.185
Wht did you have in the bag?

0:41:02.185,0:41:04.185
Talit and Tefilin.

0:41:07.240,0:41:08.839
Well, that's good.

0:41:09.480,0:41:13.799
So we arrived at the border.

0:41:15.945,0:41:17.465
First we got off.

0:41:18.584,0:41:26.664
Yes, because I had sent a baby carriage
ahead to the station, and we picked it up there.

0:41:26.904,0:41:46.705
And when we wanted to go, that woman had someone else to bring us over the border. That man says, 'Sorry, it's not happening tonight. It's not going through. The border is too dangerous.'

0:41:47.504,0:41:56.670
I said, 'Yes, I promised my in-laws and my father that I would send them a telegram from Antwerp that everything is okay.'

0:41:58.350,0:42:02.029
I said, 'We'll go to the farm.'

0:42:02.350,0:42:07.470
I said to the farmer, 'Listen, you're going to the village and you're sending the telegram'.

0:42:10.914,0:42:17.714
My wife, it was the first time in her life she was away from her parents. She wanted to go back.

0:42:19.680,0:42:21.053
She started crying.

0:42:23.139,0:42:26.820
I said, 'There is no going back'.

0:42:27.459,0:42:28.579
We're going through.

0:42:28.899,0:42:46.715
Yes, the next day, I have way too much to tell. The next day a little girl came who brought us to a certain place in the border area. There we had to wait for someone else who would pick us up.

0:42:47.514,0:42:48.394
And he didn't come.

0:42:49.259,0:42:50.940
And the girl started crying.

0:42:51.820,0:42:58.220
I said, 'You go home. We'll
stay here. We'll wait for that man.'

0:42:58.299,0:43:02.634
And that man came.
He brought us over the border.

0:43:03.274,0:43:05.035
Border... you
don't really see it like that.

0:43:05.754,0:43:12.475
And we arrived at a small station,
and there was a small train to Antwerp.

0:43:14.090,0:43:17.529
We left the baby carriage there.

0:43:20.889,0:43:27.450
We arrive in Antwerp, and I take
out the carriage and put the child in it.

0:43:29.735,0:43:33.334
And I lost the train tickets.

0:43:35.815,0:43:37.174
Do you know Antwerp?

0:43:37.174,0:43:39.414
Then it goes down
like this, the stairs going down.

0:43:43.610,0:43:47.769
Then you had to go through
control. You had to hand in tickets.

0:43:49.929,0:43:51.210
I didn't have them.

0:43:52.089,0:43:56.595
And a train came, I think from Brussels, with many people.

0:43:58.274,0:44:11.169
I went through there, and that guy, ticket, le billet, le billet in French. I roll with that little carriage down, the stairs down, and disappeared.

0:44:11.809,0:44:20.929
My wife had distant family or something she knew in Antwerp in the Jewish neighborhood.

0:44:23.225,0:44:30.264
Yes, we came here without a star.

0:44:32.424,0:44:35.544
'Yes, we just escaped from there.'

0:44:36.480,0:44:42.240
'Are you completely crazy? Walking around without stars? Do you know how dangerous that is here?'

0:44:43.200,0:44:46.559
Well, they quickly
went to buy stars for us.

0:44:46.639,0:44:50.080
Stars in Belgium are
different from in the Netherlands.

0:44:50.080,0:44:54.254
In the Netherlands it said
'Jood' and in Belgium it only said 'J'. 

0:44:56.000,0:45:06.360
Jood in Dutch and Juif in French. Also J.

0:45:11.490,0:45:16.309
We stayed there about
a week, I think. A short week.

0:45:17.509,0:45:20.470
Then we went to my
wife's cousin, in Brussels.

0:45:23.684,0:45:29.605
Our goal was to get
to so-called 'unoccupied France.'

0:45:29.605,0:45:31.486
Vichy France

0:45:31.486,0:45:40.348
We thought we might survive the war there.

0:45:43.278,0:45:46.280
We got to Brussels.

0:45:46.280,0:45:49.850
We found smugglers if you know what that is.

0:45:50.775,0:45:52.454
I won't mention the name.

0:45:56.615,0:46:10.079
There were 14 of us: 2 smugglers and 12 people, including 2 women - my wife and another woman - and our child.

0:46:11.039,0:46:22.355
And we left Brussels, not to Paris, because it was too dangerous. We had no papers with us at all.

0:46:22.355,0:46:22.914
None.

0:46:24.914,0:46:33.360
Only that personal identification and that's no good of course.

0:46:36.360,0:46:40.030
We went to south east Belgium.

0:46:40.030,0:46:41.695
With the train?

0:46:41.695,0:46:43.695
With the train.

0:46:46.732,0:46:50.335
There's a border town, Mézières Charleville.

0:46:50.994,0:47:03.589
That's one, but the twelve of us
walked over the mountains to that place.

0:47:03.590,0:47:15.154
And my wife with the child went by train that bit with a woman, Belgian or French, I don't know exactly.

0:47:16.035,0:47:19.714
We then walked, I don't
know how many hours, all night.

0:47:20.355,0:47:26.929
She went by train, and that was also dangerous, of course, because you went with that woman.

0:47:28.049,0:47:34.609
So we came to Mézières Charleville, then
we went to sleep in a hotel for one night.

0:47:34.929,0:47:46.144
The next night we went by train. If you have Brussels here, then it's southeast. Then we went back towards Paris.

0:47:49.025,0:48:04.170
We had to transfer. We stopped in Reims and
arrived in Paris on Friday afternoon.

0:48:06.325,0:48:11.764
What we didn't know was that it was
extremely dangerous. Paris was a forbidden zone.

0:48:11.845,0:48:16.325
Besides being occupied
territory, it was completely forbidden.

0:48:16.484,0:48:18.884
So if you got caught on the train there.

0:48:22.349,0:48:33.789
Sunday morning... yes, those 2 smugglers brought us to a small hotel, I don't know, in the center of Paris.

0:48:34.795,0:48:38.234
And they slept somewhere else.

0:48:40.795,0:48:44.474
By the way, there were
German soldiers in that hotel too.

0:48:47.835,0:48:56.399
Sunday morning at 7 o'clock, I look out the window, second or third floor, I don't know exactly.

0:48:56.640,0:49:06.944
There were police on all 4 corners, and everyone walking on the street was stopped. They had to show their identity card.

0:49:08.545,0:49:11.424
Not so good. How do you get out?

0:49:14.144,0:49:25.900
At 8 o'clock, a police car came, police cars, and those 4 men on the 4 corners got in and left.

0:49:27.740,0:49:32.095
5 minutes later, those
2 smugglers came. 'We're going.'

0:49:35.055,0:49:47.079
Not far, I don't remember how far it was, maybe 100 100 or 200 meters or so to the metro. We went to Gare d'Austerlitz.

0:49:49.160,0:49:51.159
Gare d'Austerlitz. It goes to the south.

0:49:53.880,0:49:59.044
We arrived at
Gare d'Austerlitz. Very few people.

0:50:00.404,0:50:05.444
Sunday morning, 9 o'clock, the train left.

0:50:05.684,0:50:09.524
We went to Tours, middle of France.

0:50:11.220,0:50:21.779
On that Sunday was the
biggest roundup in Paris, July 1942.

0:50:23.380,0:50:26.660
30 or 40,000 people were arrested then.

0:50:28.714,0:50:39.434
In Tours, we also came to a
small villa or something, all of us together.

0:50:42.400,0:50:57.724
That was about, I think, 50 or 60 kilometers from the line of demarcation, the border between occupied and unoccupied France.

0:51:01.565,0:51:06.605
We were brought there in a small truck.

0:51:09.809,0:51:18.690
Then we ended up in a small village,
or hamlet, where you'd only see German soldiers.

0:51:19.809,0:51:30.214
One of those smugglers brought us to the café, those 2 men, 'Come on in.

0:51:31.494,0:51:43.409
And they talked with young men or I don't know and they said about bringing us over the border.

0:51:44.049,0:51:50.929
Apparently that didn't work. They were drunk.
In any case, we were mainly glad we could leave.

0:51:51.184,0:51:54.704
But we still had to pay,
I believe, everyone 1000 francs.

0:51:54.785,0:51:56.865
Because we were sitting there in the café.

0:51:57.744,0:52:02.944
So that was July.

0:52:04.384,0:52:08.480
We come out of the café, in the hamlet.

0:52:09.200,0:52:15.679
And there are 14 people with a bag dressed as city people, not farmers.

0:52:18.640,0:52:20.480
And they don't know what we should do.

0:52:20.480,0:52:22.000
Those 2 men don't either.

0:52:22.575,0:52:24.014
What should we do now?

0:52:27.055,0:52:51.845
Then a couple comes towards us with a baby carriage. And one of the people who were with us, there were two brothers, twins. What were their names again, I've forgotten? They spoke good French. They came from Brussels, most were from Belgium anyway.

0:52:52.805,0:52:53.444
All of them.

0:52:55.605,0:53:01.445
And he went to that man and that woman. And he said, 'Listen, do you know where we can sleep?'

0:53:03.339,0:53:05.659
He was a Belgian farmer.

0:53:07.579,0:53:13.420
And he said, 'We are refugees from Belgium, not Jewish refugees but refugees nonetheless.'

0:53:16.194,0:53:20.114
'Come to my farm.'

0:53:22.994,0:53:30.849
We slept there. My wife and the
child and that other woman got to sleep inside.

0:53:31.009,0:53:33.089
We slept in a haystack.

0:53:43.625,0:53:48.904
We had to eat something. Bread,
milk, eggs, I don't know what, butter.

0:53:53.625,0:53:58.499
Then we said, 'Listen,
we want to cross the border.'

0:54:01.700,0:54:03.379
'Find someone for us.'

0:54:03.619,0:54:21.034
He said, 'Tomorrow evening at 10, 10:30, you have to go to that spot in the forest. Then you have to continue with that man.'

0:54:22.315,0:54:28.750
The whole day we stayed with him on the farm and then he says, 'You have to go then.'

0:54:32.990,0:54:35.789
Then he said, 'Yes, if you come with us,' because he was a bit scared.

0:54:35.869,0:54:39.964
Because there was, I think, actually a German camp.

0:54:42.045,0:54:47.540
He went with us. We came to the forest.

0:54:47.805,0:54:52.045
It was dark, a bit. It was summer.

0:54:56.669,0:55:06.509
Those 2 smugglers were again the people who did the deal. They negotiated again with that young man. They had to pay for us.

0:55:14.525,0:55:24.380
We started walking, until
4 o'clock in the morning. From 10:30.

0:55:24.940,0:55:29.579
Each time someone else carried the child. He wasn't allowed to cry.

0:55:33.535,0:55:40.654
At 4 in the morning, that boy says... he
also had signs of where to walk, how to walk.

0:55:42.174,0:55:44.494
He says, 'I'm lost.'

0:55:47.295,0:55:48.335
It was through the forest.

0:55:48.910,0:55:57.309
No road, through
the forest, fields, no path.

0:56:02.684,0:56:04.924
I thought, I need to see where I am.

0:56:06.285,0:56:10.684
Before that it was a bit like you couldn't see so well.

0:56:14.365,0:56:15.244
He came back.

0:56:18.029,0:56:21.069
We walked until 6 o'clock in the morning.

0:56:23.390,0:56:29.710
Along the way we encountered five more refugees who said, 'Just keep walking.'

0:56:30.505,0:56:38.744
We came to a large farm. There we ate and drank milk and gave butter to the child.

0:56:39.464,0:56:47.690
And that smuggler, the agreement was
that he would bring us from Brussels to Lyon.

0:56:50.169,0:56:51.769
He left us in the lurch.

0:56:53.210,0:57:02.424
I gave him another watch. Then we had to
pay part, and pay the rest when we're in Lyon.

0:57:02.505,0:57:09.970
And then he wanted a note from me that I... that we... that he brought us safely to Lyon.

0:57:09.970,0:57:12.210
'I'm not giving
that. I'm not in Lyon yet.'

0:57:14.529,0:57:21.490
'A car will come that will bring you
to the station, and from there you continue.'

0:57:25.255,0:57:26.934
Not a car. A horse cart.

0:57:28.295,0:57:55.585
We sat down and went with that little horse cart and arrived at that little station. And we got on that train, where we were twelve yes, it was a little train with 2 passenger cars.

0:57:57.184,0:58:06.480
And 5 of us went in the front part, and the others went in the second part, the second car.

0:58:10.719,0:58:13.600
You got on after being brought there.

0:58:13.600,0:58:14.400
Yes, yes.

0:58:14.400,0:58:15.679
On the horse cart.

0:58:16.960,0:58:34.054
We got on the train and suddenly we saw, the train made a stop. We saw those 7 who got into the second car being pulled out by the gendarmes.

0:58:36.454,0:58:42.679
There were 5 of us: my
wife, with my child, and 2 others.

0:58:46.600,0:58:48.119
It was something terrible.

0:58:48.119,0:58:50.600
We thought, 'Now it's our turn.'

0:58:50.600,0:58:53.880
The train went on. We arrived in Limoges.

0:58:59.684,0:59:05.284
We arrived in Limoges at the station.
A big station. Limoges is a big station.

0:59:05.284,0:59:07.759
Porcelain city.

0:59:11.520,0:59:13.200
And we wanted to go to Lyon.

0:59:15.439,0:59:31.064
Well, the man who was with us, one of the twins was there and another man. They went to get tickets for Lyon.

0:59:36.659,0:59:39.219
The train left at midnight.

0:59:45.780,0:59:49.140
You could still rent a little cushion.

0:59:50.500,0:59:56.995
The train arrives. It
was packed, packed, packed full.

0:59:58.275,0:59:59.555
To our luck.

1:00:00.674,1:00:02.834
We come in with the child.

1:00:04.915,1:00:08.950
The train has corridors.

1:00:11.590,1:00:19.349
Well, I saw that a woman with a small child... someone stood up and gave that seat to my wife and child.

1:00:19.430,1:00:23.605
We all lay there in
the corridors, on top of each other.

1:00:23.764,1:00:27.525
It was so full.
Luckily, there could be no inspection.

1:00:28.405,1:00:33.844
We arrived in Lyon at
1 o'clock in the afternoon.

1:00:35.629,1:00:37.710
So we were on the train for 12 hours.

1:00:38.750,1:00:44.750
I still don't know how we
gave our child, what we gave our child.

1:00:44.750,1:00:45.950
But I don't remember anymore.

1:00:47.310,1:00:59.694
We arrived in Lyon. There were no taxis. It was also a horse cart, what's it called, a carriage.

1:01:01.694,1:01:09.879
We had an address. It was a sister of my sister-in-law, my brother's wife who went to Chile.

1:01:10.519,1:01:18.120
They left Antwerp already
in 1940. They stayed living in Lyon.

1:01:20.424,1:01:21.944
We went there.

1:01:22.905,1:01:24.264
We had the address.

1:01:25.784,1:01:30.424
Well, she told me, 'You
have to see where you can sleep.'

1:01:30.424,1:01:51.985
So I go to restaurant Am Brun. You could find more people from Holland there. I left my wife there for a moment. I went there and I met a friend of mine, Berent Bekker, from The Hague.

1:01:52.224,1:01:58.108
I said, 'Listen, I
need somewhere to sleep.'

1:01:58.689,1:02:07.729
Go to hotel St. Pothin, the street is also called St. Pothin. Maybe he has a place for you.

1:02:07.729,1:02:09.010
So I went there.

1:02:09.409,1:02:12.824
They said, 'I'm sorry, everything is full.'

1:02:13.465,1:02:30.139
'You have to look anyway. I have a room. Those people are also from Holland. Those people are in the countryside now. They'll only come back in a week.'

1:02:30.220,1:02:34.620
'You can sleep that
week. Maybe that's what we'll do.'

1:02:35.980,1:02:40.665
And so it was. We
went there. We could sleep.

1:02:41.625,1:02:46.105
Those people came
back. There was still no place.

1:02:46.745,1:02:54.985
Those people had 2 beds
in it, and I had a baby carriage.

1:02:56.080,1:03:04.720
Those people were a couple
with a girl, 7, 8 years old, I think.

1:03:07.004,1:03:11.004
We slept for a week in this room.

1:03:11.884,1:03:12.764
Together.

1:03:15.644,1:03:19.805
Unfortunately, they've all passed
away. A daughter lives here in The Hague.

1:03:19.805,1:03:21.644
She was born here after the war.

1:03:26.889,1:03:44.084
That was the beginning when we arrived. Then the niece I told you about from Brussels also unfortunately came with her husband and son to Lyon.

1:03:46.960,1:03:51.120
And then the raids began.

1:04:01.715,1:04:18.689
Meanwhile I already had, yes, I already had the so-called passport, a pass, it was a document from the Office Néerlandais and Sally Noach

1:04:20.129,1:04:21.250
I was given that.

1:04:21.810,1:04:27.250
Also with many
miracles that I was given that.

1:04:30.024,1:04:39.705
I met someone at the top of that office and he says, 'Yes, he knows me,' because he had to testify that I'm from Holland, that I'm Dutch.

1:04:40.264,1:04:42.345
I got it.

1:04:46.810,1:04:59.665
We lived in a hotel, from there we had to leave St. Potin and we came to a hotel called Quai Jair. On the water.

1:04:59.665,1:05:00.785
Quai Jayr.

1:05:03.264,1:05:09.585
The niece also lived there, with her husband and her son.

1:05:10.145,1:05:24.000
At 1, 1:30. The National Police came. 'Open the door'.

1:05:24.000,1:05:35.367
'Papers.' First I didn't open. The whole hotel was full of refugees. Screaming. Well, I opened the door.

1:05:39.551,1:05:43.459
I opened the door. I let them in.

1:05:44.073,1:05:54.828
'Must come.' I said, 'Now I can't come, there's a one-year-old child lying here, are you completely crazy?'

1:05:55.285,1:05:58.245
'Yes, there's nothing to be done about it.'

1:05:58.245,1:06:08.245
I started screaming and luckily because of that, several police came to my room.

1:06:08.245,1:06:09.044
'What's going on?'

1:06:10.210,1:06:15.410
So I, well, I'm not leaving, I have a small child, could I speak French, I couldn't speak it anyway.

1:06:17.170,1:06:21.650
So some people could get up and run away.

1:06:24.544,1:06:30.944
We had to get up. We were
brought to a very small police station.

1:06:36.720,1:06:43.279
It was at night. At 6 o'clock in the morning, it was day. The child had nothing, hadn't drunk anything yet.

1:06:43.679,1:06:49.714
I said to that police, 'Listen, I need
some milk for this child. I need something.'

1:06:49.714,1:06:51.475
'Yes, can't do it.'

1:06:55.154,1:06:58.595
Well, screaming, so I was allowed to go with an officer.

1:06:59.315,1:07:03.990
Across the way was a milk
shop, and I got some milk there.

1:07:05.029,1:07:09.190
We went back to the station because my wife was there.

1:07:14.470,1:07:24.075
Around 3 o'clock, I think, 2
or 3, I went in a big police van.

1:07:24.075,1:07:34.129
We all had to get in
there. I think about 20 people.

1:07:38.049,1:07:44.929
And we were brought to the Sûreté. I don't know what it is. The main police headquarters.

1:07:49.225,1:08:01.300
We come in there to
the courtyard. Full of people.

1:08:04.740,1:08:24.784
Then someone came to me right away, one of those people who said to me, 'Listen, you have to go to that man there from the police with your child and your wife, because they'll let your wife and child out.'

1:08:29.080,1:08:36.759
My wife, who wanted... first situation, 'No, I'm leaving. Go to that family and tell them I'm here.'

1:08:37.799,1:08:41.480
So I go there. My wife was allowed out.

1:08:43.575,1:08:48.215
I went there. I went there. I don't
know what it is, what the street was called.

1:08:50.215,1:09:06.320
And around 7 o'clock, Saly Noah came. We
saw from a distance that we had to stay calm.

1:09:07.120,1:09:16.475
Meanwhile, at that big long
table, there were all military, I don't know.

1:09:18.465,1:09:22.993
And there in front of me was [Klaus] Barbie, sitting there.

1:09:23.454,1:09:32.325
And they asked everyone how much money you had, papers, yes, I don't know, all kinds of things.

1:09:34.230,1:09:45.795
And then people were then divided. 'Camp' in French, also 'camp de travail.'

1:09:48.915,1:09:52.779
Some were to go here and others could there.

1:09:53.019,1:09:59.339
But meanwhile we were a group of 10 or 12.

1:10:00.683,1:10:04.026
We had papers.

1:10:05.804,1:10:12.284
Now at 8, 8 o'clock
or so, somehow got out.

1:10:14.365,1:10:17.164
Came out. That was the first one.

1:10:19.269,1:10:28.549
Later we heard that camp went
directly to Drancy, went to Auschwitz.

1:10:28.789,1:10:31.429
And that camp de travail was still in France.

1:10:34.495,1:10:36.495
It was the first raid.

1:10:39.615,1:10:40.815
What should you do?

1:10:42.095,1:10:43.535
I don't know what you should do.

1:10:44.735,1:10:51.600
So I tried... yes, the
police, what they did ask, that's true.

1:10:51.600,1:10:58.559
I had to show proof
that I was leaving France.

1:10:59.834,1:11:09.834
He asked about family. I told him I have a brother in Chile who should send a telegram, that he should send a visa.

1:11:11.674,1:11:13.274
I still have the answer with me.

1:11:14.679,1:11:18.840
He writes, 'I will send
a visa as soon as possible.'

1:11:19.160,1:11:33.855
Because even if you had that, you had
to have a visa de sortie and a transit visa.

1:11:34.174,1:11:38.495
To be able to get
to Spain, and then continue.

1:11:38.894,1:11:45.129
And then I went to the
consulate of Spain and to the French.

1:11:45.209,1:11:47.209
'Yes, if you don't
have that, you can't do it.'

1:11:47.209,1:11:48.169
But well, it all didn't work.

1:11:48.169,1:11:56.490
After another week, that was September.

1:11:58.824,1:12:00.584
It was the second raid.

1:12:02.345,1:12:03.784
Still 1942.

1:12:04.105,1:12:05.464
Everything in 1942.

1:12:05.784,1:12:07.625
I'm in Lyon then.

1:12:17.090,1:12:26.129
In the second raid we weren't arrested.

1:12:26.514,1:12:31.154
My niece and my nephew and their son.

1:12:31.715,1:12:39.554
They were with us, unfortunately they went downstairs, then they found them, arrested them.

1:12:42.710,1:13:00.274
Then in the morning, it was also at night, and in the morning suddenly, at 7 o'clock I think, someone knocked on the door. It was my niece with a police officer or gendarme, I don't know exactly.

1:13:01.875,1:13:21.100
She says, 'I want to take some things with me, and give me some French money.' She gave me some valuable things of hers.

1:13:21.820,1:13:29.644
I said, 'Listen, maybe talk to your husband. We'll give you some money. They'll leave you here.'

1:13:31.085,1:13:34.524
'No, I won't do that because I'm
going back to my husband with the child.'

1:13:36.539,1:13:50.059
And she gave me the name of a police doctor who got them out the first time. 'Maybe you can try to do something.'

1:13:51.615,1:13:52.894
I said, 'I'll try.'

1:13:53.215,1:13:56.654
That was on Friday,
from Thursday to Friday.

1:13:59.774,1:14:10.549
I had no connections, but I had acquaintances from Holland who spoke good French. 'Listen, you're coming with me to that doctor there.'

1:14:15.190,1:14:16.789
The Franc wasn't worth much anymore.

1:14:21.955,1:14:26.595
'Listen, you got
that Kern family out then.'

1:14:27.075,1:14:28.274
'You'll see, they have to.'

1:14:32.009,1:14:38.250
I said, 'Do you have the money? I
don't know what you need. See what you can do.'

1:14:39.049,1:14:41.289
'I need to know where they are.'

1:14:42.730,1:14:44.009
'I don't know that either.'

1:14:45.764,1:14:47.365
'You have to find out first.'

1:14:49.365,1:15:07.350
On Saturday there was a camp
outside Lyon. It was a barracks of Indo-Chinese.

1:15:07.429,1:15:12.309
The French still had those Chinese troops.

1:15:13.924,1:15:15.444
Very large.

1:15:17.365,1:15:28.099
We came there by car with a
package, with the name Kern written on it.

1:15:30.099,1:15:33.859
Went to the
entrance with soldiers in front.

1:15:33.940,1:15:38.740
She said, 'Listen, hundreds of
people inside. I don't know how many.'

1:15:40.815,1:15:49.454
'I'll give this. Maybe if those people
are there, then I'll know the people are there.'

1:15:49.775,1:15:50.894
He took it.

1:15:57.450,1:16:08.169
Standing outside on the other side of the sidewalk were young French Jewish young men whose parents were inside.

1:16:10.194,1:16:13.315
Then I went off
and screamed at those soldiers.

1:16:13.315,1:16:22.355
You saw a few times
you saw big cars driving in.

1:16:22.355,1:16:28.009
That was a big
thing, of course, with people.

1:16:31.289,1:16:33.210
We went back.
There was nothing to be done.

1:16:33.210,1:16:37.690
The next day we heard
that they were there again.

1:16:37.690,1:17:01.440
So the next day, that was on Sunday, that roundup... the price of it all... she perished in the struggle, and the other one, my nephew with that boy, they survived until the end. They met someone.

1:17:01.519,1:17:17.254
They worked in Buchenwald, and in Buchenwald they had to march, had to go back. And that father and that son were on that road.

1:17:17.414,1:17:22.855
That father was shot dead, and that
son who lives, he still lives in Vienna.

1:17:25.619,1:17:32.339
We were young, so we had to see
about getting away, because it wasn't working.

1:17:32.659,1:17:40.339
So I had to... but you can't go to
the police or to Spain or to Switzerland.

1:17:40.845,1:17:44.204
Spain was not possible
to go over the mountains.

1:17:45.325,1:17:56.650
Switzerland too, but well, you had to have a travel permit to be able to ride the train. That was from Lyon to Annemasse.

1:17:57.049,1:18:01.210
That's the place
opposite Geneva, on the French side.

1:18:01.850,1:18:06.330
Then you had to have a permit.
You had to get that from the police.

1:18:07.325,1:18:18.525
I went to the police with a form, with a pack of cigarettes, and the next... well, in the afternoon I believe, I was allowed to pick it up.

1:18:19.244,1:18:32.980
It was written down with names and everything, and I already told you, 'Confession: Protestant.' So I could... they did check it later, of course.

1:18:34.580,1:18:41.825
And then the first day I went alone with a family. I thought, 'First let me see where I can find something.'

1:18:42.384,1:18:50.065
It was a mistake, but well, I went back to Lyon, picked up my wife and child. We went back to Annemasse.

1:18:53.570,1:18:58.369
It didn't work. That road
was broken again. Couldn't cross.

1:19:04.065,1:19:13.025
That Annemasse, that was a border town.
Just look for where, how you can get across.

1:19:15.069,1:19:23.069
I knocked on various families who lived there, Jewish families. They were all terrified that I came to the door.

1:19:24.349,1:19:32.085
We found someone who said, 'I
know someone who can bring you across.'

1:19:32.565,1:19:38.005
It cost something
like 200 francs, I believe.

1:19:44.440,1:20:03.634
We went with another family. They had a girl who was 4, 5 years old. And that man arranged, 'You have to take that bus, then you have to get off there.'

1:20:03.954,1:20:07.315
'And when you get off there, I'll
be standing there, and then we'll continue.'

1:20:10.400,1:20:14.559
We got on a bus the next day.

1:20:19.119,1:20:21.759
We got off wrong, early.

1:20:22.320,1:20:26.604
Those other people were
people who had lived in France.

1:20:28.284,1:20:31.084
Got off. We saw no one on the road.

1:20:32.125,1:20:35.244
We waited a while. Then
that man came with his bicycle.

1:20:35.244,1:20:37.085
He says, 'Why? Where are you?'

1:20:37.085,1:20:38.605
'Well, we were supposed to get off.'

1:20:42.329,1:20:52.389
We walked to the border. The Ggendarme said 'papers'.

1:20:52.581,1:20:56.038
I gave him papers.

1:20:57.734,1:21:04.295
'That's not right. Just go away.'

1:21:06.055,1:21:07.975
And I let him lead.

1:21:10.660,1:21:22.180
And that man, the smuggler... the place was called Route Nationale. This side was Switzerland and this side was France.

1:21:22.340,1:21:25.299
There it went a bit uphill.

1:21:26.045,1:21:31.005
And that man went up the
mountain with us, so not that side.

1:21:31.085,1:21:34.044
Because the gendarmes were there.

1:21:37.724,1:22:18.440
We walked maybe 10 minutes. In any case, a car came. We all had to get in, and it brought us to a post, a gendarmerie post.

1:22:20.825,1:22:31.305
And that driver of that car, the
gendarme, not the gendarmerie, was an angel.

1:22:34.660,1:22:40.419
He drove and said, 'I have
to pay that man for that ride.'

1:22:41.859,1:22:55.134
It was already dark, so I gave that man money, because he stopped waving back with that money.

1:22:57.454,1:22:58.335
He left.

1:23:01.049,1:23:03.849
Then we came to the gendarmerie post.

1:23:05.609,1:23:11.210
My wife with the child
were allowed to sleep in their house.

1:23:11.930,1:23:17.144
We were interrogated
every half hour or quarter hour.

1:23:17.785,1:23:20.585
'You wanted to
go illegally over the border.'

1:23:22.664,1:23:24.904
'You're Jews.' 
'We're not Jews'.

1:23:26.184,1:23:27.545
I had my Talit and Tfilin with me.

1:23:34.230,1:23:35.910
That went on all night.

1:23:35.910,1:23:39.509
Terrible things happened.

1:23:39.589,1:23:42.309
A woman came in with 2 daughters.

1:23:44.715,1:23:45.674
Screaming.

1:23:47.515,1:23:58.089
And 2 boys, 2 French boys, who had that woman with those 2 daughters, were supposed to bring them over the border.

1:23:58.650,1:24:01.130
They probably made
a mistake. I don't know.

1:24:01.130,1:24:03.049
In any case, they were caught again.

1:24:03.930,1:24:09.850
And that gendarme asked the boys
how much money they got from that woman.

1:24:14.585,1:24:17.144
They also beat up those 2 heads.

1:24:18.984,1:24:28.130
So they'd tell... the woman, those 2
daughters, the next day they came to Rivesaltes.

1:24:28.130,1:24:29.650
Have you heard of Rivesaltes?

1:24:30.049,1:24:33.170
Just like the transit camp.

1:24:35.330,1:24:37.330
They came from there. They had to go back.

1:24:41.724,1:25:07.400
The next day, it was in the morning, and I tried... and my wife got a little cart from the house there, so they could put the child in it. And we saw the street there. You could see the border, the barrier. And you saw the Swiss soldiers. Here you saw the French soldiers.

1:25:07.400,1:25:10.505
It was a little bit
open. There was a border.

1:25:14.721,1:25:19.840
I said to my wife. 

1:25:19.840,1:25:28.926
She has to walk with her pram as close as she could to the border, so she could try to cross with the child.

1:25:35.259,1:25:44.834
I heard one of them. there was no
road. I could speak German. He was from Alsace.

1:25:45.315,1:25:47.315
The matter was already over.

1:25:51.309,1:25:53.950
'You see, ma'am, the child is sick.'

1:25:55.150,1:26:01.230
'Maybe it's possible that the Swiss
Red Cross can take the womean and child.'

1:26:05.565,1:26:10.444
'I'll ask about it.'

1:26:10.525,1:26:14.444
And that commander got a scolding there.

1:26:16.204,1:26:20.799
'You have to lock that man up.'

1:26:21.119,1:26:30.240
I come outside. 'You come here, and
if I do anything more, I'll be handcuffed.'

1:26:33.142,1:26:41.776
They then took everything we had and all our money from us, I still have the receipt of that.

1:26:46.289,1:26:50.929
Then we were allowed to
sit in a little café and wait.

1:26:51.009,1:27:04.535
That same gendarme who stopped us the very first time came to pick us up at 3:30. We had to go back to Lyon.

1:27:04.535,1:27:10.454
On the note it said we had to
report to the police at the commissariat.

1:27:13.575,1:27:20.990
He came to us, picked us up, and
we started walking on the Route Nationale.

1:27:24.829,1:27:28.910
I saw that here,
this side must be Switzerland.

1:27:31.755,1:27:45.680
We walked a bit. I saw a house standing on the right side, and then stairs going up. Those stairs were still on the French side, and after the stairs was barbed wire.

1:27:48.799,1:27:55.039
But one piece was pushed away,
a little bit open, a little bit open.

1:27:55.680,1:27:56.720
I saw that.

1:27:57.039,1:28:03.815
I was carrying a child, with the
bag, and the other couple was also there.

1:28:04.215,1:28:07.255
And I said to
myself, 'I'm going through here.'

1:28:09.734,1:28:14.375
Because that gendarme who was just
with us, he was walking in front of us.

1:28:19.640,1:28:22.520
The barbed wire,
toward the Swiss side.

1:28:22.520,1:28:24.520
The Swiss side
and there we were met.

1:28:25.240,1:28:31.320
By a Swiss customs officer.

1:28:33.085,1:28:37.240
He brought us
to the other side of the border.

1:28:38.925,1:28:48.200
to a Swiss gendarmerie post,
or something like that.

1:28:49.440,1:28:52.960
That was around four o'clock,
quarter past four.

1:28:54.000,1:29:01.200
But it took until eight o'clock,
half past eight maybe. I don't know exactly.

1:29:01.520,1:29:05.900
before they received permission
to let us into the country.

1:29:05.900,1:29:08.930
because actually they would have sent us back.

1:29:09.170,1:29:12.300
we were lucky that we had the child with us.

1:29:12.770,1:29:16.930
otherwise they would have sent us back,
just like they sent back thousands of others.

1:29:19.820,1:29:24.620
This was September,
the beginning of September.

1:29:25.660,1:29:29.180
three days before Rosh Hashanah,
if I am not mistaken.

1:29:34.500,1:29:46.920
We were taken by car to a reception camp,
Warembé, near Geneva.

1:29:49.258,1:29:56.028
We slept in a small hall on straw.
I don't know exactly what it was.

1:29:57.040,1:30:04.000
My child was sick, so the next morning
I went straight to the commander, or whoever was in charge.

1:30:04.480,1:30:09.520
I said: listen, my child is sick,
we need, we need medical help.

1:30:12.220,1:30:23.740
We were taken by car to Geneva,
to a small hotel near the station.

1:30:31.880,1:30:41.830
We went straight to a children's home
to ask if they would take our child in for a while.

1:30:42.950,1:30:51.510
They said: we would be glad to do that,
but you must first take the child to the hospital to be examined.

1:30:52.768,1:30:59.120
He stayed there for two months,
with serious infections of all kinds.

1:31:04.810,1:31:06.320
And we were allowed to stay
in the little hotel.

1:31:08.567,1:31:11.997
That was about ten, twelve days.

1:31:12.834,1:31:14.930
During Yom Kippur
we were still there.

1:31:15.490,1:31:19.288
We came home in the evening
to the little hotel.

1:31:22.220,1:31:32.310
A Family had already told us since you are here,
you will be with us for Sukkoth. They were Swiss Jews.

1:31:35.890,1:31:48.530
I helped set up a kitchen there for the refugees to eat.
I helped with the tables and everything.

1:31:50.600,1:31:56.830
I came home and there was a letter from the police.
We had to be at the station at eight in the morning.

1:31:57.641,1:32:05.271
Cornavin station.

1:32:06.170,1:32:12.517
I went to the police and said, listen
that is not possible, our child is in the hospital.

1:32:12.922,1:32:16.976
"you still have to be
at the station in the morning."

1:32:17.680,1:32:28.797
So we went. At the station
there were 120 people waiting.

1:32:30.690,1:32:36.200
We were ordered into a train.

1:32:37.810,1:32:40.770
But first I must tell you something
about what happened in Geneva.

1:32:45.620,1:32:51.290
Suddenly, while we were at the hotel,
we received a summons to appear before the police.

1:32:54.110,1:32:59.977
I went there with my wife,
but I had to go in alone.

1:33:00.430,1:33:05.902
And then he said
you are being sent back to France.

1:33:07.400,1:33:12.019
“What is the reason?”
“You lied".

1:33:13.000,1:33:17.677
“You have deceived us.
You are not a Dutchman”.

1:33:21.680,1:33:25.653
I said, but don't you have the documents?

1:33:27.200,1:33:32.939
“We called the consulate in Geneva”

1:33:34.560,1:33:37.227
“and they said that Stemmer
is not a Dutch citizen”.

1:33:44.070,1:33:46.684
“So you have lied.
You are going back to France”.

1:33:47.680,1:33:54.930
I started shouting. My wife heard
something was wrong and came in.

1:33:55.170,1:33:58.850
"What is going on?" 
"Listen, they are sending us back."

1:34:13.020,1:34:14.940
It all came to a good end.
That was during those few days in Geneva.

1:34:16.996,1:34:24.770
We were put on the train. The train was locked
by soldiers with bayonets.

1:34:26.010,1:34:29.290
120 people locked
in one carriage.

1:34:30.480,1:34:35.190
The train rode through Switzerland.

1:34:36.000,1:34:43.680
At every transfer point they uncoupled our carriage
and connected it to another train.

1:34:45.370,1:34:50.170
So we did not have to get out.
Otherwise we might have run for it, I suppose.

1:34:50.650,1:34:54.730
We rode around until late afternoon.

1:34:56.890,1:34:59.690
We arrived in a small place
called Eriswil.

1:35:00.880,1:35:04.384
A small place. The train went no further.
That was the end of the line.

1:35:04.837,1:35:08.102
We ended up
in an old school building.

1:35:12.250,1:35:17.670
We slept there too on straw with blankets.
Our child was still in Geneva.

1:35:21.430,1:35:36.190
My wife and I were there together.
After about two months our child was brought and we fetched him.

1:35:39.280,1:35:46.400
I still have all the letters
from that hospital in Geneva.

1:35:49.440,1:35:56.770
Then the camp commander received word
that my wife and child had to leave.

1:35:57.500,1:36:06.978
They went to an internment home
called Bienenberg, near Liestal, not far from Basel.

1:36:13.210,1:36:25.467
When they arrived it turned out to be an old hotel,
with almost no heating. They were still setting it up for the refugees.

1:36:26.492,1:36:31.739
Naturally the child fell ill again right away.

1:36:31.824,1:36:37.594
In those four years, until November 1945,
he was in five or six hospitals.

1:36:38.448,1:36:40.187
Every time, always sick again.

1:36:40.650,1:36:47.835
In any case, in '43,
March or April, I don't know exactly.

1:36:48.940,1:36:55.320
I was transferred to Adliswil. 1943

1:36:56.220,1:36:59.500
From September I was there
the whole time, in Adliswil.

1:37:04.740,1:37:07.503
Adliswil is a small town
near Zurich.

1:37:10.660,1:37:16.260
I was there for a while.
I was not, no, I was not yet allowed to visit my wife.

1:37:16.820,1:37:26.650
That only came later, in the work camp.
In the work camp, in '43. I still have the documents.

1:37:29.740,1:37:34.720
I have my identity card from the Dutch consulate in Bern,
and it is also recorded in my Swiss pass.

1:37:34.720,1:37:38.540
And I also have my Swiss pass 
it's all recorded there.

1:37:38.540,1:37:41.540
It is all written there.
Somewhere end of '43.

1:37:42.580,1:37:48.778
No, no, no. It must have been
March or April.

1:37:49.870,1:37:56.639
From Adliswil I went to
the work camp Le Chaluet.

1:37:57.240,1:38:01.160
Near Moutier, in the Berner Oberland,
as we called it.

1:38:02.920,1:38:05.886
You have seen the photo.
That is Le Chaluet.

1:38:07.000,1:38:10.430
I was there for a long time.
I fell ill. I could not walk anymore.

1:38:11.390,1:38:16.840
I got muscle rheumatism.
There was no toilet there.

1:38:17.320,1:38:22.120
Just a barrack that served as a toilet.
You had to go there in the middle of the night.

1:38:23.000,1:38:31.208
It was the worst area in Switzerland.
Terrible.

1:38:32.290,1:38:38.690
The nearest village was
five or six kilometers away.

1:38:41.290,1:38:48.650
Every six weeks you got three days leave
to go and visit your wife.

1:38:50.410,1:39:01.202
You earned one franc fifty a day,
of which seventy five cents were kept back for later.

1:39:04.540,1:39:11.650
After that I was moved to
another work camp called Raron.

1:39:12.450,1:39:16.760
Near Brig, all the way south,
close to the Italian border.

1:39:20.030,1:39:30.990
Because I was ill, I got leave twice
to go to Baden near Zurich, to take the baths.

1:39:32.190,1:39:36.920
They tried everything, injections and so on.
I could not walk anymore.

1:39:40.120,1:39:44.600
Then I came to the internment camp
Morgins. Morgin that is in.

1:39:49.638,1:39:55.064
In the Valais,
right on the French border.

1:40:01.135,1:40:05.970
Only in 1944 did I manage
to bring my wife and child there too.

1:40:06.945,1:40:15.406
There were three hotels:
Grand Hotel, Victoria and Le Foret.

1:40:16.730,1:40:21.935
We were housed there,
but the child had to go somewhere else.

1:40:21.935,1:40:26.924
That did not work at all.
He stopped eating. It was terrible.

1:40:29.000,1:40:38.613
He was in hospital again.
Lausanne, Zurich, Baden, Geneva.

1:40:41.550,1:40:46.950
And so it went on,
until the liberation came.

1:40:50.550,1:40:56.087
We still could not go home.
The liberation was May 1945.

1:40:57.990,1:41:05.470
There too we were interned.
We listened to the English radio.

1:41:10.497,1:41:15.707
I was not allowed to return to the Netherlands,
because I was not a Dutch citizen.

1:41:23.980,1:41:30.660
To tell it briefly,
we arrived on my birthday, November 7th, 1945.

1:41:32.453,1:41:41.359
By train from Basel,
through France, back to Holland, to Eindhoven.

1:41:41.620,1:41:45.470
In the factory hall of Philips
we slept there for a night. We registered.

1:41:47.470,1:41:54.490
I still have those papers.
Displaced person, I don't know what. 

1:41:54.490,1:41:56.190
I still have all those documents.

1:41:59.179,1:42:07.328
We had no house.
In Eindhoven at that time was the Jewish Brigade.

1:42:12.540,1:42:24.940
We had a good acquaintance who had a non-Jewish wife.
He was in Westerbork but got out because of the mixed marriage.

1:42:26.020,1:42:34.580
He lived in Amsterdam on the Rijnstraat
and wrote to us right after the war, when you arrive, come to us.

1:42:40.350,1:42:49.396
Meanwhile we also had a second child.
He was born May 29th, 1945.

1:42:52.020,1:43:03.380
In Switzerland, in Montreux.
We were up in the hills above Montreux.

1:43:06.540,1:43:08.540
so she had to go
to a hospital.

1:43:10.300,1:43:11.724
So, you went to Amsterdam?

1:43:12.620,1:43:16.240
No, my wife went to Amsterdam
with the two children. I went to The Hague.

1:43:16.240,1:43:18.940
And I went to The Hague
to see what was going on.

1:43:22.810,1:43:27.040
The city hall was gone.
It was now in the Goudenregenstraat.

1:43:27.770,1:43:32.410
I said, I used to live here,
and I was forced to leave.

1:43:35.140,1:43:48.580
Before we fled I had dealt with the man
who became the owner. My house had been sold during the war.

1:43:52.340,1:43:58.159
I said, "but I used to live here,
and I had my business here."

1:43:59.206,1:44:03.580
They said, you cannot go back there.
You will just have to find somewhere else to live.

1:44:05.420,1:44:10.700
There was nothing. I met some people
and slept where I could.

1:44:13.360,1:44:18.550
On Prinsmauritslaan there was a house
that had been managed by the Jewish community.

1:44:19.670,1:44:24.171
I slept there. Then I got a room
in the city, on the Hoefkade.

1:44:27.260,1:44:34.060
And then there was the house of my parents-in-law,
who had been taken away in September 1942.

1:44:34.780,1:44:39.630
They were taken by that Judenfischer,
Franz Fischer, that murderer.

1:44:41.790,1:44:50.430
Because my sister-in-law was sitting on a bench
with the twins, two girls of three years old.

1:44:51.390,1:44:59.234
In the Belgisch Park, you know it.
Fischer walked past and arrested them.

1:44:59.450,1:45:07.290
In any case, the whole family,
my parents-in-law, their three daughters and the twins,

1:45:08.090,1:45:10.445
were brought to the Windekind.
You know it too. They stayed there one night.

1:45:10.445,1:45:14.065
You'll know it.
They stayed there for one night.

1:45:14.070,1:45:18.540
I have proof of that.
Someone who visited them there told me.

1:45:19.900,1:45:25.920
They were classified as a punishment case.
Within ten days they were dead in Auschwitz.

1:45:28.240,1:45:32.080
That was my wife's story.
That is why I do not want to say more about it.

1:45:32.960,1:45:40.207
I came back. Not long after
I received a notice from the Ministry,

1:45:42.724,1:45:50.490
Military Affairs or something, stating that I owed
a certain amount for the return journey from Basel to Eindhoven.

1:45:52.430,1:45:52.930
Very kind indeed.

1:45:56.170,1:45:56.270
I went there and said:
Listen, I have nothing. I don't even have a place to live.

1:45:56.270,1:46:01.710
I went there and said, "listen,
I have nothing. I don't even have a place to live."

1:46:02.190,1:46:02.290
They said, if you do not pay,
you will be undesirable and sent back to Switzerland.

1:46:02.290,1:46:07.975
They said, If you don't pay,
you'll be an undesirable person and sent back to Switzerland.

1:46:13.730,1:46:19.351
I said, I did not say I do not want to pay.
I said I cannot pay right now.

1:46:22.300,1:46:28.700
So we drew up a contract:
I would pay one guilder a month.

1:46:32.510,1:46:35.080
A few weeks later I got a letter.
They had made a mistake.

1:46:35.630,1:46:41.550
It was not, it was not that much, it was not 500 guilders.
It was 500 Swiss francs.

1:46:41.950,1:46:44.740
And at the time the franc was worth less
than the guilder, so I owed less.

1:46:45.760,1:46:45.860
Still, I paid the full amount
a few months later.

1:46:45.860,1:46:49.300
Still, I paid the full amount
a few months later.

1:46:53.540,1:46:55.695
Well, I think I have covered it
more or less.

1:47:00.160,1:47:04.143
My father, my father and my sister.

1:47:05.168,1:47:15.247
My sister, in April, in April,
with four children, was taken to Sobibor.

1:47:17.910,1:47:26.740
My father was on the last transport.
That was August, August 1943.

1:47:26.740,1:47:29.610
No, 43.

1:47:29.610,1:47:34.204
No no. Sobibor was closed.

1:47:37.020,1:47:43.580
Yes, yes. Not to Sobibor,
because Sobibor was shut down by then.

1:47:45.340,1:47:52.210
That was the last transport.
I believe that is what happened to my father.

1:47:54.300,1:48:00.450
I don't know if you know it,
the 17th of Tammuz, a Jewish fast day.

1:48:01.020,1:48:10.700
On that day all the chief rabbis of The Hague,
all the other rabbis, were deported to Sobibor.

1:48:11.580,1:48:14.620
You can read it all
in that book about Sobibor.

1:48:19.990,1:48:21.110
Did you then move into that house?

1:48:21.990,1:48:29.510
No, I had to go and live in that house,
but that, well, that did not just happen.

1:48:29.980,1:48:35.340
After the war a property management institute
had been set up.

1:48:36.620,1:48:40.300
I was told to go there,
so I went.

1:48:41.100,1:48:46.510
"Oh, is that you? We have already appointed
an administrator for you. We assumed you were not coming back either".

1:48:48.009,1:48:52.089
He gave me the name.
The administrator was a teacher in Rijswijk.

1:48:53.070,1:49:00.480
We contacted him and he was very glad
that at least someone came back and he could hand it over.

1:49:02.960,1:49:07.680
He had been managing both
my parents-in-law's property and mine.

1:49:08.160,1:49:13.770
But the house was uninhabitable.
There was no glass in the windows.

1:49:14.090,1:49:20.960
That man arranged it. He was head of a school, I think.
He got glass,

1:49:22.570,1:49:27.440
from the horticultural trade. The glass was installed,
and the house was made a little livable.

1:49:27.440,1:49:32.100
The house was made a little livable,
and we got some furniture.

1:49:32.100,1:49:34.240
We got some furniture. At that management institute
you could get furniture. That is how we started.

1:49:34.240,1:49:39.220
There was that management institute
where you could get furniture. That's how we started.

1:49:44.963,1:49:48.742
On the right is my father David Loeb Stemmer

1:49:51.133,1:49:55.098
On the left side, you can see my mother, Augusta Stemmer-Better.

1:49:57.745,1:50:04.357
My mother was about thirty years old when this was taken.

1:50:11.146,1:50:23.913
Look. The photo of my father was taken here in Holland, around 1935, 1936.

1:50:30.617,1:50:40.085
Here you can see my brother, second from the left.

1:50:40.555,1:50:48.618
All the way on the right is my sister-in-law.
They went to Palestine in 1935.

1:50:48.618,1:50:51.436
And in front of him is his son.

1:50:55.668,1:51:04.170
I am standing all the way on the left.
Next to me is a small boy, their son.

1:51:07.885,1:51:13.958
He is Professor Shamir from Jerusalem. And behind him stands my father. 

1:51:15.025,1:51:20.526
And my sister and my brother-in-law.

1:51:33.464,1:51:39.331
Yes, my mother is here, and my father is carrying the youngest on his arm.

1:51:42.277,1:51:45.984
Yes, that is the professor in Jerusalem. 

1:51:48.119,1:51:53.310
The other ones now also live in Jerusalem.

1:51:53.310,1:51:59.404
No, not all of them, some perished in Sobibor. The youngest one is my sister’s.
